[Music]  a new school year is getting underway  but that doesn't always mean children  returning to the classroom  in fact the majority of the nation's  large school districts have opted for  remote learning instead  some parents are taking things into  their own hands forming what are known  as pandemic pods they're a version of  homeschooling for small groups of  children  sometimes led by teachers or tutors but  as more companies pop up to organize  these pods a debate is brewing about  equity  meg oliver reports okay remember you can  use adding subtracting multiplying and  dividing whatever you want  all summer in this quiet backyard under  a canopy of trees  these four students have tackled math  english and social studies three days a  week  so thinking back through your entire  summer what was like the most awesome  part of it  this private education pod in larchmont  new york is just  one of many popping up across the  country ten-year-old bianca arinsibia is  going into fifth grade  it helps me because i have  much more attention than i do in regular  school  yeah and do you like coming to your pod  i don't know  when school shut down last spring  bianca's mom julia couldn't keep up with  the stressful demands of remote learning  with three kids  i found it really challenging to go from  physics with the older one  to you know tapping out sounds to learn  how to read for the kindergartner it was  two very different sets of skills did  you feel like you were treading water at  times that's how i felt the whole time  she turned to schoolhouse a service that  matches families with teachers for  in-home  education i think it's safe to say we  hit our five-year business goals in  about five months  co-founder joseph connor launched the  company in january  with eight students in a pod the cost is  about fourteen thousand dollars per  child  per year how is this different than  homeschooling so it's different because  we provide professional teachers we  recruit them from  all over the country a lot of them come  from private and charter schools  generally our teachers can make anywhere  from 10 to 20 percent more than what the  local  average salary is it's an attractive  model for parents but one that could  lead to fewer  students in local school districts and  less funding for the children  left behind our schools are more diverse  than they have ever been  but they're still segregated our pods  have an opportunity unfortunately to  become even more segregated  larue louis mccoy is a new york  university sociologist who studies  educational inequality  he worries about vulnerable communities  including the estimated 17  million kids without high-speed internet  access  at home the families that have more  money are actually creating  boutique almost private school  environments for their children  the danger in that is that as these  children move far  ahead that many of their classmates and  many of the people who will later be  comparing them to as they apply to  college  their life looks very different it's  something entrepreneur kate hahn says  is on her mind we have no interest in  taking funding away from schools  we want to work with them and in fact  we've reached out to a number of schools  in my local school district for example  han and doug  shachtel both co-founders of pricing new  york private schools  teamed up for a company called learning  pods over the summer we decided  you know we need to have a way to bring  joy to kids so the idea of  having summer pods where you have a  small group of kids  meeting with camp counselor who brings  curriculum with them  and they meet in a family's backyard and  that was really successful  the company kicks off its fall program  later this month  parents of kids in kindergarten through  fourth grade can expect to pay around 15  thousand dollars for the academic year  how are you looking to make this  affordable for everybody so a part of  every tuition  goes towards financial aid in addition  we're working with foundations  to end personal donations to feed  that same financial aid fund a good  start but not enough to go around for  the many children in need says lewis  mccoy the magnitude  of disadvantage that public schools deal  with cannot be  compensated for with private partnership  families have to do things sometimes  like reach across  socioeconomic lines you may have to  cross a neighborhood  you may have to talk to a family that  you haven't spoken to before but if you  do that work we begin the process of  inclusion  do you feel like this could be the wave  of the future as we  you know try to navigate through this  pandemic absolutely  so we really think that this is not just  a solution for the present we think that  this is the best way  for teachers to teach and students to  learn we're also working with  some philanthropic groups to make sure  that we have sponsor pods  and we're actually also trying to work  with some state governments on offering  these pods through government programs  a complex problem that many parents are  thinking about  including julia aaron sebia i think it's  a horrible situation i wish it weren't  the case  and i think that you know at the end of  the day i need to  do whatever i can for my children and  frankly if i can keep my children at  grade level  then they won't need services that can  go to another child so by taking care of  my children and their needs in some ways  it's helping everybody  for cbs this morning saturday mega  oliver larchmont  new york one of the two million issues  created by coronavirus in schools and  we've talked about this  as parents about how each district is  handing things differently  it's tough yeah and i understand you  want to do everything you can for your  child  but that economic divide just hits me in  a way that's right  so unfair right the appeal is definitely  there as a parent you can understand the  need  and the desire to do this but fourteen  thousand dollars that just seems  incredibly out of reach for so many  people  and again the have nots even less have  even less right now during the pandemic

